WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.786
Eight card payments from March, at four merchants.

2
00:00:05.786 --> 00:00:09.241
Finance wants each payment next to its merchant's total.

3
00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:14.609
GROUP BY merchant adds up each merchant's payments, and folds them into one row.

4
00:00:14.609 --> 00:00:16.679
Eight rows go in, four come out.

5
00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:18.241
The payments are gone.

6
00:00:18.241 --> 00:00:23.301
A grouped row stands for a whole merchant, so there's no payment id left to show.

7
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.670
A window function keeps every row.

8
00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:29.053
Write OVER after the sum, with PARTITION BY merchant.

9
00:00:29.053 --> 00:00:31.925
Rows with the same merchant form a partition.

10
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:39.104
For each row, the database adds up the row's window, here its partition, and writes the sum into a new column.

11
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:47.106
The window of payment 17 holds payments 17, 18 and 19, so its total is 56,997.

12
00:00:47.106 --> 00:00:52.711
Rows 18 and 19 have the same window, and the same total.

13
00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:55.306
Every row gets its merchant's total.

14
00:00:55.306 --> 00:00:57.339
Eight rows in, eight rows out.

15
00:00:57.567 --> 00:01:00.208
Now add ORDER BY created at, inside OVER.

16
00:01:00.208 --> 00:01:04.137
The rows that a window function adds up are called its frame.

17
00:01:04.137 --> 00:01:07.357
With ORDER BY, the frame stops at the current row.

18
00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:11.185
At merchant 5, payment 13 adds up only itself.

19
00:01:11.185 --> 00:01:15.196
Payment 16 adds two rows, and payment 22 all three.

20
00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:16.886
That's a running total.

21
00:01:16.886 --> 00:01:22.697
The database sorts each partition once, then carries the sum down: one addition per row.

22
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:27.301
Lag reads just one row of the window: the row before the current one.

23
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:33.816
Payment 17 is first at merchant 4, so it has no row before it, and lag gives null.

24
00:01:33.816 --> 00:01:36.665
For payment 18, the row before is 17.

25
00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:40.495
Payment 18 came two minutes after 17, for the same amount.

26
00:01:40.495 --> 00:01:45.231
Put each payment next to the one before it, and a double charge stands out.

27
00:01:45.433 --> 00:01:47.891
GROUP BY folds each group into one row.

28
00:01:47.891 --> 00:01:51.420
A window keeps every row, and lets it see its partition.
